    "created_at": "2021-09-06T17:37:26Z",
    "updated_at": "2021-09-06T17:37:26Z",
    "body": "It's uncommon, sure, but it's also uncommon to have a CLI tool where new features added aren't discoverable. If `exa` adds new fields to display, they'll just show up. If `starship` adds new modules, and I've set the top-level `format` config key, then I'll never know about the new modules without periodically checking the documentation. And even if I do that, I won't know what was added since I last checked as there's no changelog and no way to even know what version I last checked my configuration for unless I explicitly write it down.\r\n\r\nHeck, even if I don't touch `format`, any new modules that are opt-in won't be obvious. Or any new features of existing modules that are opt-in.\r\n\r\nSide note: I just checked the [latest release](https://github.com/starship/starship/releases/tag/v0.57.0) in response to this issue and discovered by scrolling down to \"Features\" (Why is this not the first section of the changelog?) that pipestatus was finally implemented. This is something I've wanted for a long time but only found out by happenstance right now. And I have no idea what version I was on when I created my current config so I don't know how far back to even read in the releases list to find out what else I've missed.\r\n\r\n> Having them in the program itself is only going to create unnecessary maintenance burden.\r\n\r\nNot really. The program can just learn to [query GitHub](https://docs.github.com/en/rest/reference/repos#list-releases) for releases, which doesn't even need authentication.",
